menu "lua-os board"

# Which physical board this image is for.
#
# Not a cosmetic label: a peripheral's pins are board facts, and probing
# for one that is not there is not harmless. Bringing up the T-Deck's
# shared SPI bus on a machine without it hangs before the kernel starts
# -- which is what the SD probe did to every qemu boot test, silently,
# because a probe that answers "no" and a probe that never returns look
# identical from outside.
#
# So the default is NONE: a board says what it has, and anything else
# gets a machine with a console and nothing else.

choice LUAOS_BOARD
	prompt "Target board"
	default LUAOS_BOARD_NONE

config LUAOS_BOARD_NONE
	bool "none -- emulator or a bare ESP32-S3"
	help
	  Console only. No peripheral probe runs, which is what makes
	  this safe under qemu.

config LUAOS_BOARD_TDECK
	bool "LilyGo T-Deck"
	help
	  8MB octal PSRAM, 16MB flash, microSD on the SPI bus shared
	  with the display and the LoRa radio.

config LUAOS_BOARD_CARDPUTER
	bool "M5Stack Cardputer"
	help
	  No PSRAM: the lua heaps come out of internal SRAM, so this is
	  the tight machine. Display and keyboard are not wired yet.

endchoice

config LUAOS_PRECOMPILE
	bool "ship embedded lua as stripped bytecode"
	default n
	help
	  Compile every embedded module with luac -s instead of shipping
	  source. Measured at 11703 resident bytes per chunk against 14894
	  from source, and it loads about 16x faster -- but the bigger win
	  is the parse peak, which is what actually fails first: loading
	  from source needs the source, the parser's state and the
	  resulting prototypes live at once.

	  It costs the debugger's line numbers in those modules, so a
	  traceback from lib/ names a chunk and not a line. That is why it
	  is a choice and not the default.

	  Modules that cannot be parsed on this machine at all are always
	  precompiled regardless -- see PRECOMPILED in main/CMakeLists.txt.

config LUAOS_FB_SHADOW
	bool "keep a copy of the framebuffer, so fb.unload works"
	default n
	help
	  This panel cannot be read back -- the Cardputer's LCD connector
	  is eight pins and the ST7789's SDO is routed nowhere -- so the
	  only unload possible is from a copy the driver keeps.

	  Costs 64800 bytes of internal SRAM at 240x135, which on a board
	  with no PSRAM is a large fraction of what is free. It exists so
	  tools/screenshot-esp32.lua can see what the guest drew; turn it
	  off for anything that has to fit.

endmenu
